3":"to(wards)","H5674":"to pass","H518":"if","H3426":"there","H4341":"pain","H5953":"to abuse","H2740":"burning anger","H639":"anger","H4791":"height","H7971":"depart","H784":"fire","H6106":"bone","H7287":"to rule","H7568":"net","H7272":"foot","H8076":"devastated","H1739":"sick","H8244":"to bind","H5923":"yoke","H8276":"to intertwine","H5927":"rise","H6677":"neck","H3782":"to stumble","H136":"Lord","H3201":"be able","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H5541":"to reject","H47":"ox","H7130":"among","H7121":"call to","H7665":"to break","H970":"youth","H1660":"wine press","H1869":"to tread","H428":"these","H589":"I","H5869":"eye","H4325":"water","H7368":"to remove","H1121":"child","H1396":"to prevail","H3290":"Jacob","H5439":"around","H5079":"impurity","H6662":"righteous","H6310":"lip","H4784":"to rebel","H8085":"hear","H4994":"please","H1992":"they(masc.)","H7411":"to shoot","H2205":"elder","H1478":"to die","H6887":"to confine","H4578":"belly","H2560":"to aggitate","H2015":"to overturn","H3820":"heart","H2351":"outside","H7921":"be bereaved","H2719":"sword","H1004":"place","H4194":"death","H7451":"harmful","H7797":"to rejoice","H859":"you(m.s.)","H6213":"do","H3644":"like","H585":"sighing","H1742":"faint"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","City. David had conquered many. Jerusalem was long considered as the finest city in those parts. — Tributary. It had been so to the Assyrians, Egyptians, and Chaldeans, 4 Kings xxiv. 1. From this and similar passages, it would seem that the city was still existing: yet in others it appears to have been demolished. Here then the prophet declares what it had been: (Calmet) unless he wrote part after the death of Josias. (Haydock) — The beholders are astonished at the change and misery of the city. (Worthington)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Night; privately, or without ceasing. — Friends, who had made a league with Sedecias, chap. xxvii. 3., and xlviii. 26."],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Rest. Many returning to join Godolias, chap. xl. 7. (Calmet) — The Jews who beheld their brethren led away to Babylon, retired into Egypt, but were in misery. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Feast, thrice a-year. This was the most charming sight, when all the nation met to adore God, and to renew their friendship with one another. (Calmet)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Lords. Literally, “at the head,” (Haydock) which Moses had threatened, Deuteronomy xxvi. 1, 43. (Calmet) — This would be most cutting. (Worthington)"],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Beauty; princes’ palaces, but particularly the temple, ver. 10. (Calmet) — Rams, fleeing from place to place to seek relief. (Worthington)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Of all. She compares her past happiness with her present chastisement. — Sabbaths, or days of rest. The pagans derided them as so much lost time. Ignava et partem vitæ non attigit ullam. (Juvenal v.; Seneca, apud St. Augustine, City of God vi. 11.) — If none of their legislators thought of such an institution, it was because they had not the spirit of Moses: their feasts were dissolute. (Calmet)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Unstable. Hebrew also, “removed,” (Haydock) like a woman unclean. (Calmet) — Such were excluded from places of prayer, and were not allowed to touch a sacred book, or to pronounce God’s name. Their husbands could not look at their face, nor give them any thing, but laid it down for them to take. (Buxtorf, Syn. 31.) — No condition could be more distressing. (Calmet)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","End in her prosperity, to avert this misfortune. (Haydock) — Idolatry is a spiritual adultery, (Worthington) and one of the worst species of filth. (Haydock)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Church. Deuteronomy xxxiii. 1., and Ezechiel xliv. 9. The Chaldeans disregarded the ordinance."],["haydock",11,12,"haydock","O. Hebrew of the Masorets, “It is.” (Calmet) — Protestants, “Is it nothing to you, all?” &c. (Haydock) — But the Vulgate is much clearer, and approved by many Protestants, lu being often used as an exclamation, Genesis xvii. 18. (Calmet) — Vintage. He has plundered all, ver. 22. (Haydock) — The king took a great deal, and his general the rest, 4 Kings xxiv., and xxv. (Worthington)"],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Bones: fortresses. (Theodoret) — I am like one in a burning fever, Ezechiel xxiv. 4. (Calmet) — Chastised. Literally, “instructed.” This is the good effect of affliction. (Haydock)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Watched. This metaphor is not too harsh, chap. xxxi. 28. The Masorets prefer, (Calmet) “is bound by his hand.” (Protestants) But miskad is explained (Haydock) by the Septuagint, &c., in the sense of the Vulgate. God lays the yoke on my neck suddenly. My iniquities are like bands, and Nabuchodonosor has power over me."],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Mighty. Hebrew, “magnificent” princes, (Luke xxii. 25.) or warriors. — Time of vengeance. All is animated. Hebrew also, “a troop” of Chaldeans, chap. ii. 22. — Juda. God, as the first cause, punishes the Jews by war."],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Then. They surround the city, to starve the inhabitants, ver. 8."],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","Me. Egypt attempted to relieve Juda, to no purpose, ver. 2. (Calmet) — It could not, or at least did not, prove of any service to the Jews, chap. ii. 18. (Worthington)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Alike, by famine, &c. (Calmet) (Worthington) — Ubique pavor et plurima mortis imago. (Virgil, Æneid ii.)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Done it. They conclude that I am cast off for ever. But when I shall be comforted, their turn will come; (Calmet) or rather they will feel the scourge soon after me. — Consolation. Hebrew, “which thou hast appointed.” (Haydock) (Chap. xlviii. 26., &c., and Ezechiel xxv., &c.)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Let. He prays not for their ruin, but predicts it; and wishes rather that they would be converted. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[1,2,0,"Letters","Letter 53",0,420,"As for Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel and Daniel, who can fully understand or adequately explain them? The first of them seems to compose not a prophecy but a gospel. The second speaks of a rod of an almond tree and of a seething pot with its face toward the north, and of a leopard that has changed its spots. He also goes four times through the alphabet in different meters."],[1,2,1,"The Instructor Book 1",null,1,215,"… So your ears are uncircumcised. O blessed forbearance! And again, by the same: \"All the heathen are uncircumcised, but this people is uncircumcised in heart,\" \"for the people are disobedient children,\" he says, \"in whom faith does not exist.\" … Bewailing one's fate is latent censure and artfully helps to bring salvation, albeit under stealth. He made use of this by Jeremiah: \"How did the city sit solitary that was full of people! She that ruled over territories became as a widow; she came under tribute; weeping, she wept in the night.\" … In the end, the system God pursues to inspire fear is the source of salvation. …"],[4,null,2,"Funeral Oration on Meletius","FUNERAL ORATION ON MELETIUS",2,395,"… But now our wound is beyond healing; our Elijah has been caught up, and no Elisha left behind in his place. You have heard certain mournful and lamenting words of Jeremiah, with which he bewailed Jerusalem as a deserted city, and how among other expressions of passionate grief he added this, \"The ways of Zion do mourn. These words were uttered then, but now they have been realized. For when the news of our calamity shall have been spread abroad, then will the ways be full of mourning crowds, and the sheep of his flock will pour themselves forth, and like the Ninevites utter the voice of lamentation, or, rather, will lament more bitterly than they. …"],[16,null,3,"Register of Epistles","Book 1",3,604,"… Know then that I am stricken with so great sorrow that I can scarcely speak, for the dark shades of grief block up the eyes of my soul. Whatever is beheld is sad; whatever is thought delightful appears to my heart lamentable. For I reflect to what a dejected height of external advancement I have mounted in falling from the lofty height of my rest. And, being sent for my faults into the exile of employment from the face of my Lord, I say with the prophet, in the words, as it were of destroyed Jerusalem, \"He who should comfort me has departed far from me. …"],[16,20,4,"Concerning Repentance","Book 2",4,397,"… We notice that he thought this the bitterest addition to his woes, that he who used to comfort the mourner was gone far from him. How, then, can you take away the very comfort by refusing to repentance the hope of forgiveness? But let those who repent learn how they ought to carry it out, with what zeal, with what affection, with what intention of mind, with what shaking of the inmost bowels, with what conversion of heart: \"Behold,\" he says, \"O Lord, that I am in distress; my bowels are troubled by my weeping; my heart is turned within me. …"]],"fau":[["jerome","Jerome",1,"father",null,1,420],["clement-alexandria","Clement of Alexandria",0,"ecclesiastical-writer",null,1,215],["gregory-nyssa","Gregory of Nyssa",0,"father",null,1,395],["gregory-great","Gregory the Great",1,"father",null,1,604],["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 6 (1893)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf206"],["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 2 (1885)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf02"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 5 (1893)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf205"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 12 (1895)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf212"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"]]}